{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before dawn, when the world hasn't decided what kind of day it will be yet. The sky is a bruised purple, the air is cool enough to make you pull your sleeves down over your hands, and every sound feels borrowed. It's in that quiet hour that my mind finally stops shouting and starts whispering.\n\nThere is a small bakery on the corner of 5th and Elm that opens at four. The baker, an old man named Milo, never says much. He just nods, slides a warm croissant across the counter, and goes back to kneading dough like he's shaping time itself. I've sat on the bench outside his shop more mornings than I can count, watching the streetlights flicker off one by one as the sun takes over their shift.\n\nI think we all have a Milo in our lives \u2014 someone or something that anchors us without demanding anything in return. For some it's a place, for others a song or a dog-eared book that smells faintly of vanilla. Mine happens to be that bench, that croissant, that brief ritual of being awake while the city is still dreaming.\n\nIt reminds me that you don't need grand adventures to feel alive. You just need a moment that belongs entirely to you, a small pocket of stillness you can return to whenever the world gets too loud. And if you find it, hold onto it tightly. Let it be yours forever more.",
